University of  Kent to provide the Step Up to Social Care educational services in accordance with the requirement of Kent County Council&#039;s (KCC) which includes:

1.	Provision of appropriate educational services inline with the Professional Standards for Education and Training&quot;, for a maximum of 20 students to produce high caliber social workers conversant with the necessary Knowledge and Skills Statements as set out by the Chief Social Worker, utilizing both theoretical classroom-based education and practice placements. 

2.	To ensure methods of teaching are carried out in a hybrid approach of in person and virtual learning opportunities.

3.	Create, manage and execute, in partnership with the Council and its partners, an agreed admissions process, including DBS checking;

4.	To achieve and maintain any and all standard both internal and external to the Provider required to provide the services, inline with &quot;Social Work England Education and Training Standards 2021&quot;

5.	To provide Practice Educators for a maximum of 20 students for both 70 and 100 day placements and ensure that all expectations of their role are carried out including appropriate reporting of students progression of their learning and work via their assessments of their capability at each stage;

6.	To undertake the programme within 14 months;

7.	To ensure that the services are provided by appropriate, qualified staff at all times;

8.	To provide all appropriate, agreed and otherwise necessary and reasonably expected student support services;

9.	To provide all agreed and other reasonable required reports

10.	To maintain the minimum service levels.

This service is for a partnership between Kent County Council and Medway Council to deliver social worker training that will produce qualified social care workers. Kent holds the lead responsibility in the partnership. 

Funding for the service for Kent and Medway Council is fully provided in the form of a grant from the Department for Education (DfE). 

This procurement was carried out using a competitive procedure with negotiation and was advertised on the 8th March 2023  via the Kent Business Portal.

This opportunity was advertised on the 8th March 2023 under reference KENT001-DN649906-06017295 on contracts finder

Kent County Council (KCC) has awarded a contract to provide independent financial appraisal reports to support the investment decisions of the KCC managed business investment schemes.

The key requirements of the Service are as follows:

a) to attend to 2-3 monthly decision meetings on request;

b) to prepare at the full application stage for each eligible applicant of the KCC managed business investment schemes, a financial appraisal report. This report should contain business and financial analysis of the investment proposal and be submitted via KCC to the monthly decision meetings;

c) to prepare at the debt recovery stage for existing loan recipients of KCC managed business investment schemes, a financial appraisal report. This report should contain business and financial analysis of the current financial position of the business to assist in the understanding of the current financial position of a company. This report to be submitted via KCC to the monthly decision meeting;

d) Each financial appraisal report should include a range of information to assist the monthly decision-making meeting make its Recommendations i.e., to Partially Fund/Fully Fund/Reject/Defer, suggest conditions, repayment schedule and security (if funding was to be offered);

e) there may be an ad-hoc requirement for follow-up independent appraisal work connected with the KCC managed business investment schemes.

Most of this work can be undertaken remotely but attendance at designated Investment Advisory Board (IAB) or Subgroup (SG) meetings and the Debt Recovery Group meeting are also a requirement for the contractor.

The contract may be extended for two further periods of twelve months.

Kent Community Health NHS Foundation Trust (KCHFT) are looking to appoint a contractor for the refurbishment of four consulting rooms and a waiting room with associated services including supply and installation of fire alarm, access control and call assistance at Molehill Copse Clinic, Maidstone, Kent, ME15 7NB.

Bidders must be able to demonstrate all of the following at the time of bidding and for the duration of the construction works:
•	Must be Constructionline registered with minimum &#039;Silver&#039; verified status;
•	Must be NICEIC or NAPIT registered;
•	Must be BS7671 18th Edition IET Wiring Regulations certified;
•	Must hold BAFE SP203-1 Certification; 
•	Must have successfully carried out similar types of work at healthcare (NHS or Private), or other comparable &#039;live environments&#039; within the last 5 years.

Site visit dates will be held on the 8th December,2022 at 1100 hrs with an alternative being held on the 12th December, 2022 at 1100 hrs.

Type of Contract:
JCT Intermediate Building Contract with Design (2106 Edition)

Works are anticipated to commence February 2023, with completion by the 31/03/2023.

KCHFT would currently like potential suppliers to express their interest in this opportunity which will be completed via the Proactis e-procurement portal. Once suppliers have expressed their interest, all ITT Documents will be sent to them via the Proactis portal on the 05/12/2022. Any correspondence to the Authority&#039;s staff email addresses will be disregarded.

If you are not already registered on the Proactis Portal, please see the following link to register: https://procontract.due-north.com/Register. The Due North project ID for this opportunity is: DN644099.

*This is for information only, as the contract has been awarded* 
Following completion of this tender process, West Kent proposes to enter into a Contract with the successful Supplier for an initial period of three (3) years plus up to two (2) one-year extensions at West Kent&#039;s discretion and performance depending.
The scope of services will include:
•	General technical support and advice for all passenger, light goods and disabled access lifts / hoists queries; 
•	Specification writing; 
•	Project management and maintenance contractor performance monitoring, including leading on regular review meetings;
•	Site surveys, auditing and inspections; 
•	Examination, condition and dilapidation reports; 
•	Compliance assurance.

There will also be a requirement to support West Kent&#039;s Property team with the procurement of its third-party lifts maintenance contractor(s) during the second half of 2024.

The full details of the services are set out in the specification.
